SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I haven't slept in three days because I'm terrified they will take away my support, and my body is completely breaking down from the stress of proving I'm sick.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of extreme bureaucratic exhaustion from repeated justification of basic needs and anticipatory access anxiety preceding any interaction with institutional access offices. Over the past 72 hours, facing an impending accommodation review, the patient has experienced complete sleep deprivation and a physical exacerbation of their underlying disability symptoms. The patient describes feelings of profound dehumanization from proving diagnostic severity to administrators, chronic frustration, and the development of a defensive, combative communication style out of necessity. They report diminished professional output secondary to time spent securing accommodations and a past reluctance to request adjustments leading to self-imposed physical harm.
Functional Status: Currently functionally impaired due to extreme sleep deprivation and acute exacerbation of underlying disability symptoms.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of functional collapse, falls, and worsening of baseline disability due to systemic exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/88 | HR: 100 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Patient appears disheveled, highly agitated, pacing initially but quickly fatiguing. Speech is pressured and defensive.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Mild tachycardia, normal heart sounds. Respiratory: Clear breath sounds bilaterally. Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. Tremor noted in bilateral upper extremities consistent with severe fatigue and adrenaline. Cognitive assessment reveals poor concentration and scattered thought processes driven by anxiety over impending accommodation reviews. Musculoskeletal: Evidence of baseline disability (e.g., restricted range of motion in lower limbs) which the patient states is subjectively much worse today due to the physical toll of advocacy.
Environmental Observations: Dining table is covered in extensive, disorganized medical files, forms, and highlighted policy documents.
Medication Reconciliation: No changes to baseline disability medications. Patient admits to using excessive caffeine to stay awake to organize paperwork.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Disability Accommodation Navigation Fatigue - Adjustment Disorder - V65.49 (Problem related to unspecified psychosocial circumstances) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Primary Manic Episode - Ruled out/unlikely due to the agitation and lack of sleep being strictly tied to a specific, threatening environmental stressor (the review) rather than spontaneous, grandiosity, or typical bipolar cycling.

Substance-Induced Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as, despite high caffeine intake, the core symptoms and physiological collapse are disproportionate to caffeine alone and explicitly linked to bureaucratic exhaustion.

Acute Delirium - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient remaining fully oriented to time, place, and person, despite the cognitive scattering from severe sleep deprivation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute physiological collapse, severe agitation, resting tachycardia (HR 100), and a 72-hour period of complete insomnia driven by the systemic trauma of navigating disability accommodations. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to exclude an acute organic delirium, manage the acute severe anxiety, and provide immediate pharmacological intervention for sleep to prevent a complete physical crash or psychiatric emergency. Delaying care until routine hours would have exacerbated the sleep deprivation to a dangerous level, definitively warranting urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a single dose of oral temazepam 10mg to forcefully break the insomnia cycle. Advised usual GP to review stress biomarkers including salivary alpha-amylase and psychometric profiling showing high institutional distrust, and to provide medical certification specifically for 'administrative fatigue'.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage bureaucratic exhaustion, implement a "compartmentalization strategy": dedicate only one specific hour (e.g., 10 AM - 11 AM) strictly for administration tasks, physically packing the paperwork away outside this hour; successfully maintaining this boundary will reduce the pervasive sense of threat. To mitigate dehumanization by proof, utilize an advocacy proxy (a trusted friend or professional service) to attend the upcoming review meeting; delegating this task will directly reduce the anticipatory access anxiety and physical toll.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a dedicated disability advocacy service or social worker to manage bureaucratic load.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for ongoing sleep management and formalizing medical support documentation for the accommodation review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing acute confusion, hallucinations, inability to stand or walk, or sudden onset of chest pain.

